A||||6||JohnnyK||Upper A-arm pivot bolt removal||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||07-08-2000||05:39 PM||||I need to replace the upper a-arm bushings, but I cannot seem to get that long pivot bolt out.  Whenever I twisted it with a wrench, it went, then sprung back like an elastic band, so I kept twisting it (assumed it was seized to the bushings), but I still can't get it to come out. I've taken the shock off and smacked the end of the bolt with a hammer, but still no luck. The rest of the a-arm isn't attached, so there is no pressure anywhere. Is there something I'm missing, or do I just have to put a socket on the end and hope that when I turn it, it grips into something and starts to come out? thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||07-08-2000||05:49 PM||||Often, the bolt and the sleeve rust together. When you turn it, the sleeve just rotates inside the rubber bushing. Spray everything down good with penetrating oil. Put the nut back on until the face of the nut and the end of the bolt are flush. Get a hefty hammer and whack it and then turn it, repeatedly. Continue doing this for an hour until it finally comes loose or until your arms are too tired.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-08-2000||07:07 PM||||What Batboy says is the only way. Generally once you can get the bolt to move left and right, It takes about 10 minutes and LOTS of penetrating oil (WD40, Liquid Wrench, ETC)<BR>I've had to do it twice, once on my car and once to a friends car. Both times averaged about an hour a side.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||wilburt||07-08-2000||08:49 PM||||On an '86, (lower A arm), I pounded on that bolt so much that I was distorting the mounting tabs. Finally used a Sawzall to cut both ends of the bolt off,(next to the bushing) and it fell right out. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||07-09-2000||03:48 AM||||But in theory, if it were not seized and new, it would just slide in and out? Doesn't have to be screwed in or anything?||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||07-09-2000||08:25 AM||||Yes, if it was all new, it would just slide in and out. With all that rust in there, you have to penetrating oil it, turn it, and pound on it. Try a little heat from a torch.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||07-09-2000||08:46 AM||||Also when you change everything and go to put it back together coat the bolt with a silicone grease. This will help it from corroding to the sleeve again. Don't use a petrolium based grease it will hurt the bushings (rubber or poly)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-09-2000).]||||reg||1
